BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20260923T011055Z
DTSTART:20260929T140000
DTEND:20260929T150000
SUMMARY:SBIR/STTR Webinar: Cracking the Code: NIGMS SBIR Priorities and W
 hat It Takes to Get Funded
TZID:America/Chicago
CREATED:20260905T010000Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T010000Z
DESCRIPTION:National Institute of General Medical Sciences (NIGMS) suppor
 ts basic research that increases our understanding of biological process
 es and lays the foundation for advances in disease diagnosis\, treatment
 \, and prevention. Where most other NIH institutes fund research tied to
  a specific disease\, organ system\, or population\, NIGMS takes a diffe
 rent approach\, supporting fundamental research into the principles and 
 mechanisms that underlie living systems themselves\, often using researc
 h models that serve as windows into those general principles. That disti
 nction is exactly what makes NIGMS a natural fit for so many SBIR applic
 ants working on foundational science or platform technologies\, but it a
 lso means many aren't sure how to position their proposal to align with 
 what the agency is actually looking for. Understanding NIGMS's prioritie
 s\, and how to frame your application to speak to them\, can make the di
 fference between a proposal that gets funded and one that doesn’t. Join 
 us for a live webinar led by Eddie Billingslea\, Ph.D. from NIGMS\, as h
 e walks through NIGMS's current priorities and what it takes to put toge
 ther a competitive SBIR proposal. This webinar will cover:NIGMS prioriti
 es relevant to SBIR applicants\, and how to tailor your specific aims an
 d application language to align with themWhat reviewers and program offi
 cers look for in Phase I and II proposals\, including common strengths\,
  weaknesses\, and pitfalls that lead to triage or declineHow and when to
  engage program officers effectively\, including best practices for cont
 acting program directors and managers\, and what materials to shareLive 
 Q&A with our NIGMS speaker  Whether you're developing the next therapeut
 ic\, diagnostic\, or research tool grounded in basic biological science\
 , this is your chance to get funding insights directly from the person w
 ho manages NIGMS's Small Business program oversees SBIR/STTR support. Br
 ing your questions\, bring your ideas\, and let's find your mechanism of
  action.   About the Speakers Eddie Billingslea\, Ph.D.\, is the Small B
 usiness Strategy Coordinator at the National Institute of General Medica
 l Sciences (NIGMS). He is responsible for coordinating the NIGMS small b
 usiness program\, as well as identifying and developing new opportunitie
 s for NIGMS commercialization and entrepreneurial activities\, especiall
 y those related to capacity building\, training\, and mentoring. Prior t
 o joining NIGMS\, Dr. Billingslea was the Strategic Planning and Evaluat
 ion Lead in the Office of Research on Women’s Health (ORWH). Prior to th
 at\, he was a Health Science Policy Analyst in the Center for Research S
 trategy at the National Cancer Institute (NCI). Dr. Billingslea earned a
  B.A. in psychology from Virginia Union University and a Ph. D in neuros
 cience from Georgetown University. He completed postdoctoral fellowship 
 in neuropsychiatry at the University of Pennsylvania.This is a free\, vi
 rtual event\, but registration is required. The Zoom link to the webinar
  will be emailed to you upon completion of your registration.  Recording
  Notice: By registering for this webinar\, you acknowledge and agree tha
 t it will be recorded and shared online for public viewing. For more inf
 ormation and how to access more resources\, visit the FAST Center at Ill
 inois website.  The Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) and Small 
 Business Technology Transfer (STTR) programs are highly competitive prog
 rams that encourage domestic small businesses to engage in Federal Resea
 rch/Research and Development (R/R&D) with the potential for commercializ
 ation. Through a competitive awards-based program\, SBIR and STTR enable
  small businesses to explore their technological potential and provide t
 he incentive to profit from its commercialization. By including qualifie
 d small businesses in the nation's R&D arena\, high-tech innovation is s
 timulated\, and the United States gains entrepreneurial spirit as it mee
 ts its specific research and development needs.
LOCATION:https://forms.illinois.edu/sec/699547774
CATEGORIES:Webinar
CONTACT:Illinois FAST Center
ORGANIZER:il-fast-training@lists.illinois.edu
URL:https://calendars.illinois.edu/detail/5115/33561685?key=2000010120000
 10133561685
UID:33561685@illinois.edu
END:VEVENT
